      sources: "Sources: Learn, certificateBasedAuthPki; certificateAuthorityDetail list; x509 get; strengths; CA policies; legacy CBA config; Set up CBA. Chapter 05, “The check.”",
      say: `The read needs three permissions, each the least-privileged for its call: one to read the public key infrastructure, one to read the authentication method policy, and one to read policies, for Conditional Access. The code reads the newer PKI trust store on purpose, because the older configuration's delegated read names only Global Administrator. One judgment call: a root CA with no CRL is a warning, not a finding, because a root that issues only CA certificates may publish none. Learn's prerequisite is wider, so Lab 2 confirms the root's revocation path.`,
      detail: `The read needs three permissions, each the least-privileged for its call. List certificateBasedAuthPki, and each one's CAs: PublicKeyInfrastructure.Read.All; Authentication Administrator or Privileged Authentication Administrator. Get the X.509 certificate method: Policy.Read.AuthenticationMethod; Global Reader or Authentication Policy Administrator. List authentication strength policies: Policy.Read.AuthenticationMethod; Conditional Access Administrator, Security Administrator or Security Reader. List Conditional Access policies: Policy.Read.All; Global Reader or Security Reader. The code reads the PKI-based trust store, not the older organization/{id}/certificateBasedAuthConfiguration, whose Learn pages name Global Administrator as the only role for a delegated read. A root CA with no CRL configured is a Warn, not a Finding: a root that issues only CA certificates may publish none. Learn's prerequisite is wider, "each CA has a CRL," and Learn says Entra checks the CRLs up the chain to the root, so the warning asks you to confirm the root's revocation path, and Lab 2 checks it (verification log, row 14).`,
