      say: `Step three checks the fictional tenant for GCC, with its twenty-million-byte limit. Five findings and seven warnings, and the exit code is two. Read the story in the fixture. Issuing CA 2 was renewed, so the thumbprint the card program expects is gone from the trust store, and the renewed CA's CRL is large, slow and served over HTTPS. A legacy intermediate has no CRL at all. The mode rule maps the wrong OID, so PIV is single-factor. And the phishing-resistant policy is still report-only.`,
      detail: `Step 3, check the fictional tenant for GCC: python python/cba_check.py check --cloud gcc --exports tests/fixtures/ch05/exports --crl tests/fixtures/ch05/crl-measurements.csv --expected config/cba-expected.json --as-of 2026-11-01 --out out/ch05/check. Cloud gcc: interactive CRL limit 20,000,000 bytes, download within 10 s. CBA: 5 Finding, 7 Warn, 0 Info. Findings: CB-01 the Legacy Intermediate has no CRL configured; CB-04 PIV Issuing CA 2 (renewed): CRL took 11.4 s, the limit is 10 s; CB-09 PIV policy OID 2.16.840.1.101.3.2.1.48.11 not mapped to multifactor, and the default is single-factor; CB-10 no enabled policy for all users and all resources requires a phishing-resistant authentication strength (SCuBA MS.AAD.3.1v1); CB-12 PIV Issuing CA 2 isn't in the trust store. Warnings: CB-01 the root CA has no CRL; CB-02 the renewed CA's endpoint is HTTPS; CB-05 its CRL is 18,500,000 bytes, over 80% of the gcc limit; CB-08 CRL validation isn't enabled, and one CA is exempted; CB-11 the Legacy Intermediate expires 2026-12-15; CB-13 PrincipalName binding configured. The slide shows a pick of the lines. The exit code is 2. The story: CA 2 was renewed, so the thumbprint the card program expects is gone (CB-12) and the renewed CA's CRL is large, slow and on HTTPS. The tenant's mode rule maps the wrong OID (CB-09), and the phishing-resistant policy is still report-only, so the enforced policy requires only the MFA strength (CB-10).`,
