      sources: "Sources: Learn, Entra CBA Certificate Revocation List. Chapter 05, “Dangers.”",
      say: `The dangers, and the guard for each. A CRL that grows past the limit takes down every card from that CA, so the size rule and its early warning run every time. A slow or unreachable endpoint causes the same outage from the network side. A CA without a CRL lets revoked cards sign in. A renewed CA nobody added locks out new cards. A troubleshooting exemption that stays turns revocation off. PIV mapped as single-factor means the card isn't what meets MFA. And a report-only policy is a requirement only on paper.`,
      detail: `A CRL that grows past the limit: every card from that CA fails sign-in; guard: CB-03 and the early warning CB-05, every run. An unreachable or slow CRL endpoint: same outage, from the network side; guard: CB-04; Learn's highly available HTTP endpoints. A CA without a CRL: revoked cards still sign in; guard: CB-01. A renewed CA not added: new cards don't sign in, and nobody knows why; guard: CB-12 by thumbprint, CB-11 before expiry. A troubleshooting exemption that stays: revocation is off for that CA; guard: CB-08 lists every exemption, every run. PIV mapped as single-factor: users meet MFA with a password plus something else, not the card; guard: CB-09. A phishing-resistant policy left in report-only: the requirement exists on paper; guard: CB-10 counts only enabled policies. Measuring from inside the PKI's network: the measurement passes while Entra's path fails; guard: run measure from an internet-facing vantage point (design guidance).`,
